# 7. Practical Finding: Expired SSL/TLS Certificate

One finding identified during the lab was an expired SSL/TLS certificate.

Example:

```text
SSL/TLS: Certificate Expired
```

Example lab details:

```text
Severity: 5.0 Medium
Host: 172.17.0.1
Port: 443/tcp
```

## Meaning

The certificate presented by the service had passed its validity period.

## Remediation

Replace the expired certificate with a valid certificate.

### Memory

```text
Expired certificate
        ↓
Replace / renew certificate
```

---

# 8. Practical Finding: TCP Timestamps

OpenVAS also identified the use of TCP timestamps.

TCP timestamps are an optional TCP feature used for functions such as round-trip measurement and protection against certain sequence-number issues.

Depending on the environment, timestamp information can potentially provide information that helps estimate system uptime.

Example lab severity:

```text
2.6 Low
```

---

## Linux Configuration

The lab demonstrated:

```text
net.ipv4.tcp_timestamps = 0
```

The configuration can be applied using:

```bash
sysctl -p
```

---

## Windows Configuration

The lab also demonstrated:

```cmd
netsh int tcp set global timestamps=disabled
```

The exact availability and behavior of TCP timestamp configuration can vary between Windows versions.

### Memory

```text
TCP timestamps
      ↓
Potential information disclosure
      ↓
Review whether timestamps are required
```

